## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an end-to-end data governance workflow with real-time drift detection across tenants, leveraging Data Catalog tagging, BigQuery taxonomy, and alerting. Focuses on correctness, security, and scalability trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Data taxonomy in BigQuery and how to surface it for tagging
- Data Catalog tagging API for dynamic resource labeling
- Drift detection patterns in streaming pipelines
- Idempotent processing guarantees in Dataflow and BigQuery inserts
- Observability and alert routing to Cloud Run/Slack and BigQuery summaries

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: apply a Data Catalog tag to a resource
const {DataCatalogClient} = require('@google-cloud/datacatalog');
const client = new DataCatalogClient();
async function tagResource(resourceName, tagTemplate, fields) {
  const [tag] = await client.createTag({
    parent: resourceName,
    tag: {
      template: tagTemplate,
      fields: fields
    }
  });
  return tag;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle taxonomy evolution without breaking existing tags?
- What metrics would you monitor to balance false positives vs drift detection latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests basic streaming ingestion, Pub/Sub, Cloud Functions, and BigQuery interaction; checks ability to implement idempotent writes with insertId; ensures knowledge of minimal IAM and basic test planning.

## Key Concepts

- Pub/Sub ingestion and ordering
- Cloud Functions for schema validation
- BigQuery insertId for deduplication
- IAM minimally scoped
- Test plan including duplicates and invalid messages

## Code Example

```javascript
const {BigQuery} = require('@google-cloud/bigquery');
const bigquery = new BigQuery();

exports.ingestSensor = async (message, context) => {
  const data = Buffer.from(message.data, 'base64').toString();
  let parsed;
  try { parsed = JSON.parse(data); } catch (e) { return; }
  if (!parsed.device || typeof parsed.ts === 'undefined' || typeof parsed.value !== 'number') {
    return;
  }
  const insertId = `${message.id}_${parsed.device}_${parsed.ts}`;
  await bigquery.dataset('iot').table('readings').insert([Object.assign({}, parsed, {insertId})]);
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency and ensure dedup when messages are out of order?
- How would you adapt this to handle schema evolution changes or late-arriving data?

